t-hoc labeling function mapping a full trajectory to a distribution over 𝒵 {\mathcal{Z}} , with z ^ ∼ P L ( ⋅ ∣ τ ) \hat{z}\sim P_{L}(\cdot\mid	au) as the sampled control (note that P L P_{L} and z ^ \hat{z} are strictly ℱ T \mathcal{F}_{T} -measurable). This training paradigm constructs learned dynamics converging to P sim ​ ( u t ∣ h t − 1 , z ^ ) ≜ P π b ​ ( u t ∣ h t − 1 , z ^ ) P_{	ext{sim}}(u_{t}\mid h_{t-1},\hat{z})	riangleq P^{\pi_{b}}(u_{t}\mid h_{t-1},\hat{z}) . Crucially, this implicitly anchors the simulator to a learned prior P sim π b ​ ( z ^ ) = ∫ P L ​ ( z ^ ∣ τ ) ​ d ​ P π b ​ ( τ ) P_{	ext{sim}}^{\pi_{b}}(\hat{z})=\int P_{L}(\hat{z}\mid	au)dP^{\pi_{b}}(	au) , inextricably coupling the control variable’s distribution to the specific actions of the behavior policy π b \pi_{b} . Example: Post-Hoc Labeling in Practice. Suppose a simulator is trained on support logs where an annotator assigned static traits like z ^ = “easily frustrated” \hat{z}=	ext{``easily frustrated''} . Crucially, if the behavior policy π b \pi_{b} provided unhelpful answers, it actively caused this frustration. Thus, the label is not an intrinsic user trait, but an artifact of the specific agent’s actions. Policy Dependence of the Control Variable. Because a trajectory τ 	au is inextricably tied to the behavior policy π b \pi_{b} , unlike a static, ℱ 0 \mathcal{F}_{0} -measurable prior, z ^ \hat{z} cannot be disentangled from the agent’s actions. Assessing our structural divergence metric W T ​ ( z ) W_{T}(z) exposes how this entanglement breaks the evaluation on two distinct fronts. First, regardless of the evaluation policy , conditioning on a post-hoc label creates a backward causal path from future to past actions (see Figure 1 ), injecting a strict look-ahead bias ( Section 3.1 ). Second, when a new evaluation policy ( π e ≠ π b \pi_{e}
